# ==============================================================================
# Does the library still survive Jellyfin's HLS stream-copy path?
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE CHECK FOR A FAILURE THAT HAS ACTUALLY HAPPENED, AND IT RAN BY HAND
# ONLY. bin/verify-media.sh existed for weeks and no unit anywhere referenced
# it. What it catches is a file whose keyframes are closer together than the 6s
# HLS segment length: ffmpeg merges consecutive short GOPs until it has six
# seconds, so segment N holds different media than playlist entry N from the
# second segment onward and the error ACCUMULATES - measured on one film at
# +3.838s after one segment and +22.397s after twenty-five. What somebody
# watching sees is the picture jumping forward and then subtitles that no longer
# match, and the symptom names neither the cause nor the file.
#
# AND THE POPULATION GROWS EVERY DAY. Tdarr writes into library/transcoded
# continuously - 725 files at the time this was written, 63 films and 662
# episodes - so a check that runs when somebody remembers it is a check whose
# coverage falls every week.
#
# SAMPLED, NOT --full. Three 120s windows per file, reading packet keyframe
# FLAGS, which is pure demux: `-skip_frame nokey` would decode, and on a spindle
# that difference is the whole cost of the script. Reading every library file
# end to end is the operation that took this host down once already.
#
# THROTTLED BECAUSE THE SPINDLE GETS SLOWER WITH CONCURRENCY. /mnt/media is one
# 7200rpm disk where two readers cost 45% of total throughput and the penalty is
# head travel rather than layout - so the answer to contention here is fewer
# jobs, never more bandwidth. Nice, CPUWeight and IOWeight are the same trio
# home-server-verify-restore.service and the CI sweep carry, and `io` IS
# delegated to the user manager on this host - host.io_delegated asserts it,
# because every IOWeight in stacks/ was inert until it was.
#
# ExecCondition= AND NOT ExecStartPre=, which is the whole reason the gate lives
# in the script under --gate. A non-zero ExecCondition is "skip quietly" and
# leaves the unit NOT failed; an ExecStartPre failure turns an ordinary
# deferral into a red unit. bin/update-when-idle.sh made exactly this choice for
# exactly this reason.
#
# A DEFERRAL IS WHY media.verify_run GRADES THE MARKER AND NOT THIS UNIT. A
# skipped run CLEARS ExecMainExitTimestamp rather than leaving it stale, so
# check_timer_run would report "has never run" and FAIL from the first
# deferral, on a host that swept the library perfectly a week earlier.
#
# TimeoutStartSec= AT NINETY MINUTES. 725 files at three demux windows each,
# serialised, on a disk that is also serving whatever else wants it. The
# observed run is well inside that; the ceiling exists because a Type=oneshot
# blocked forever sits in `activating`, which host.failed_units cannot see - it
# is neither failed nor a container.
#
# EXIT 1 MEANS TWO THINGS and the marker is what separates them: a completed
# sweep that found drift, and a die() that could not run at all. The script's
# header says so; media.keyframe_drift reads media_error rather than the code.
#
# NO Before= AND NO Wants= ON ANYTHING - not even on jellyfin, which this
# reaches into. A Wants= would start the container to run a check about it, and
# the 2026-08-30 outage was an ordering cycle systemd broke by deleting a job
# with every container healthy and nothing failed.
#
# Symlinked into ~/.config/systemd/user/ - see host/systemd/README.md.
# ==============================================================================

[Unit]
Description=Verify the transcoded library's keyframe grid
Documentation=file:/var/home-server/bin/verify-media.sh

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecCondition=/var/home-server/bin/verify-media.sh --gate
ExecStart=/var/home-server/bin/verify-media.sh --library \
          --marker %h/.cache/home-server/media-state
TimeoutStartSec=90min

Nice=10
CPUWeight=20
IOWeight=20
MemoryHigh=256M
MemoryMax=384M
